WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.820
3.7 million hectares lost legal logging in 1992, scattering cuts into flood-prone watersheds. This

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:13.060
is unintended consequences, episode 179. President Corazon Aquino faced accelerating

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:18.840
forest loss that had already reduced cover to roughly 18% of the national land area. In

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:25.400
October 1988, she imposed attacks on loggers to fund replanting. In March 1989, she banned

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:32.620
log exports. In May 1991, her administration issued DAO-24, shifting all commercial cutting

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:38.880
out of old growth stands and into residual or plantation forests. The June 1992 National

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.320
Integrated Protected Areas System Act further locked selected primary forests inside reserves.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.600
These steps followed the visible damage from typhoon urine in November 1991,

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:55.340
when logs and debris from cleared slopes contributed to the deaths of at least 4,000 people in

00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:00.600
Ormock. Aquino and her advisors treated the removal of large-scale operators as the

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.540
direct way to keep soil on slopes and water in channels. They had watched decades of

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.780
export-driven cutting reduce the country's once abundant primary hardwood stands,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.280
and reason that ending legal access to those stands would let forests recover while shifting

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.840
any remaining harvest to already disturbed second-growth areas. The sequence of measures therefore

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:25.560
aimed at both immediate protection of Virgin Timber and long-term watersheds stability.

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:30.513
DAO-24 took effect on schedule in January 1992.

00:01:31.353 --> 00:01:37.713
By 1996, the number of Timber license agreements had fallen from 97 concessions to 36 concessions

00:01:37.713 --> 00:01:40.173
covering 1.56 million hectares.

00:01:40.813 --> 00:01:46.033
The government canceled several large operations, including the ACME plywood concession in the Sierra

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:51.293
a moderate after documented illegal cutting. Proponents pointed to the drop in official log

00:01:51.293 --> 00:01:56.813
production as evidence that pressure on Virgin Stans was easing. Skeptics inside the Forest

00:01:56.813 --> 00:02:01.873
Management Bureau noted that cancelled concessions were not being placed under effective protection,

00:02:02.453 --> 00:02:07.533
and that roads built for the former operations remained open. The reduction removed roughly

00:02:07.533 --> 00:02:13.493
2.2 million hectares from licensed commercial use within six years, yet the same road networks

00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:18.573
that had once carried company trucks now stood available for any smaller operator who could reach them.

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:23.933
Because the policy focused on canceling licenses rather than closing or patrolling the access

00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:29.693
routes, the physical infrastructure of extraction stayed in place even as the legal operators departed.

00:02:30.759 --> 00:02:36.019
Ernesto S. Guiang's Food and Agriculture Organization review later documented that the policy

00:02:36.019 --> 00:02:41.599
displaced rather than reduced harvest pressure. Lowland families followed those logging roads into

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:45.019
the Uplands, where more than 20 million people eventually lived.

00:02:45.479 --> 00:02:50.639
In the northern Sierra Madre, Yoshiki Seki recorded that ex-logging workers and migrants from

00:02:50.639 --> 00:02:54.639
Ifugau turned former concession land into open access zones.

00:02:55.139 --> 00:03:00.519
Small-scale operators with chain saws moved into residual forests that the 1992 rules had

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:02.219
left outside protected boundaries.

00:03:02.899 --> 00:03:06.739
Commercial logging had accounted for roughly 10% of annual forest loss.

00:03:06.739 --> 00:03:13.139
The remaining 90% came from agricultural expansion and slashing burn once the large firms departed.

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:18.379
Because total demand for timber and fuel would stayed constant, the new cutters simply

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:20.859
worked smaller patches across more water sheds.

00:03:21.419 --> 00:03:26.839
Headwater soils eroded faster, increasing silt loads that reach low land rice fields and coastal

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:27.419
fisheries.

00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:31.019
The same pattern repeated after each new provincial moratorium.

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:36.939
Leagle operators left, illegal chainsaw crews stayed, and the forest edge continued to retreat.

00:03:37.559 --> 00:03:41.899
Roads and markets created under the earlier concession system continued to lower the cost

00:03:41.899 --> 00:03:44.919
of reaching remote stands for anyone with portable equipment.

00:03:45.479 --> 00:03:50.139
When large firms exited, the firms left behind both cleared patches ready for planting

00:03:50.139 --> 00:03:55.179
and an established network of buyers who still needed wood, so the economic incentive

00:03:55.179 --> 00:03:57.319
simply moved to the next available actor.

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:02.739
The result was a wider scattering of cuts rather than a smaller total volume, with each

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:06.919
new site contributing incremental erosion that accumulated in downstream channels.

00:04:07.479 --> 00:04:12.079
Over time, the open access condition meant that even community members who had once supported

00:04:12.079 --> 00:04:17.179
the ban on outsiders found themselves drawn into the same small-scale cutting once the corporate

00:04:17.179 --> 00:04:18.199
presence disappeared.

00:04:19.253 --> 00:04:26.393
Subsequent administrations added further restrictions, including the 1995 Executive Order 263, on

00:04:26.393 --> 00:04:32.393
community-based forest management, yet annual forest loss remain near 47,000 hectares according

00:04:32.393 --> 00:04:39.273
to forest management bureau data. Later presidents, including Rodrigo Duterte in 2018, issued fresh

00:04:39.273 --> 00:04:44.953
regional bans after typhoon-driven landslides. The underlying shift from corporate concessions to

00:04:44.953 --> 00:04:50.453
disperse small-scale cutting has never been reversed. Enforcement remains weakest precisely where

00:04:50.453 --> 00:04:55.453
the original policy opened access roads and left residual stands without clear tenure.

00:04:55.453 --> 00:05:00.213
year. Each new ban therefore encountered the same geography of fragmented holdings and

00:05:00.213 --> 00:05:05.856
mobile cutters that the 1992 measures had helped create. When a national rule removes

00:05:05.856 --> 00:05:11.256
large, visible operators from an open resource, it rewards whoever can reach the same resource

00:05:11.256 --> 00:05:17.136
with smaller, harder to detect equipment. The 1992 ban on old growth concessions lowered

00:05:17.136 --> 00:05:22.696
recorded production while increasing the number of entry points into headwaters. Any later

00:05:22.696 --> 00:05:27.256
Another restriction that leaves roads, markets, and would demand unchanged will again favor

00:05:27.256 --> 00:05:30.116
the most mobile cutters over the most regulated ones.

00:05:30.676 --> 00:05:35.856
Future forest rules must address both the legal status of the land and the daily economics

00:05:35.856 --> 00:05:37.876
of the people already living on its edge.

00:05:38.396 --> 00:05:43.096
A cancelled license does not close a road, and a road that still reaches a buyer still sets

00:05:43.096 --> 00:05:44.396
the price of a day's cutting.

00:05:45.136 --> 00:05:49.556
Listeners tracking how land rules meet the people already on the ground can follow that same

00:05:49.556 --> 00:05:51.376
pressure on environmental intelligence.

00:05:51.376 --> 00:05:56.836
How might a similar policy change today alter the daily choices of the people already positioned

00:05:56.836 --> 00:05:58.036
along those same roads?

00:05:58.576 --> 00:05:59.796
That's the case for today.

